\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Topic shortcut confound.}
Three observations argue against a topic-level shortcut: (1) topic classification yields unstable OOD generalization (F1\,=\,0.541$\pm$0.207 on DD); (2) scrambled labels match correct labels (F1 gap $\leq$3.9\,pp); (3) MLM achieves strong detection without labels (F1\,$\geq$\,0.97 at $K \leq 5$). Domain exposure, not topic-level classification, drives detection.

\paragraph{Single-LLM confound.}
Both training and synthetic OOD data are generated by Qwen3-8B. ToxicChat (204 human-written jailbreaks) confirms the adapted-vs.-vanilla gap transfers to human data (+18--42\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:human_data}); cross-LLM experiments validate transfer when the benign distribution is held constant (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}).
Mixed-LLM 9-class maintains strong DD detection (F1\,=\,0.818$\pm$0.096 vs.\ vanilla 0.291$\pm$0.065, +53\,pp) while MLM performs at vanilla level (0.289$\pm$0.101), but AA degrades (F1\,=\,0.504$\pm$0.107, +11.5\,pp over vanilla). This is condition-specific: single-source training achieves AA F1\,$\geq$\,0.91 (Tables~\ref{tab:evidence_hierarchy},~\ref{tab:scale_validation}); the mixed-source gap likely reflects limited per-source volume (250 per LLM) rather than a method limitation.

\paragraph{Variance reversal on human data.}
On synthetic OOD, classification supervision reduces cross-seed variance 4--20$\times$ over MLM; on ToxicChat, this reverses (std\,=\,0.087 vs.\ 0.013), indicating MLM's advantage is specific to single-source conditions.

\paragraph{Encoder capacity confound.}
Our three encoders (125M--355M) span a 2.8$\times$ capacity range but also differ in pretraining and tokenization; the capacity-dependent observation (Section~\ref{sec:cross_encoder}) requires same-architecture scaling to confirm.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

On synthetic multi-turn data, three findings emerge. First, for base-size encoders (125--184M), classification-based DAPT is the most robust adaptation; unsupervised pretraining closes $\sim$60\,pp on single-source data but collapses on mixed-LLM training, while the largest encoder (355M) generalizes without adaptation. Second, fewer attacks require adaptation as model size grows. Third, classification supervision stabilizes variance (4--20$\times$) on synthetic data but reverses on human data (ToxicChat MLM F1 0.837 vs.\ 9-class 0.596).

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Multi-turn human validation.}
All multi-turn findings are derived from synthetic conversations generated by Qwen3-8B. ToxicChat validates transfer to human-authored single-turn attacks (+18--42\,pp over vanilla), but no human-generated multi-turn jailbreak dataset exists for evaluation. Whether the evidence hierarchy, capacity-dependent observation, and classification supervision advantages generalize to human-authored multi-turn attacks remains the primary open question.

\paragraph{Binary detection head.}
All classification variants include both a binary jailbreak detection head and a strategy classification head. Binary vs.\ 9-class performance equivalence (F1 gap $\leq$3.9\,pp) suggests the binary head alone is not the critical differentiator, but a strategy-only variant (without binary head) would be needed to fully disentangle their contributions.

\paragraph{Dataset scale and diversity.}
The training set uses synthetic conversations generated by Qwen3-8B. We validate robustness to dataset scale by doubling the training corpus from 500 to 1{,}000 conversations: classification-based DAPT maintains strong OOD generalization (9-class DD F1\,=\,0.889$\pm$0.041, ActorAttack\,=\,0.913$\pm$0.041), and the hierarchy 9-class $\gg$ MLM $\approx$ vanilla holds (DAPT advantage 40--56\,pp over vanilla). The perturbation evaluation ($n$=76) remains underpowered; tier boundaries in Table~\ref{tab:perturbation} correspond to 1--3 misclassifications. All multi-turn data is synthetic. ToxicChat (human-authored, single-turn) confirms transfer across data sources (+18--42\,pp over vanilla; Section~\ref{sec:human_data}), but whether the full evidence hierarchy generalizes to human-generated multi-turn attacks is open.
Cross-LLM validation with Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct as the target model reveals that jailbreak detection transfers across LLMs (F1 recovers to 0.80--1.00 when the benign distribution is held constant), but the benign baseline is LLM-specific: replacing Qwen benign text with Llama benign text causes false-positive rates to spike (F1 drops to 0.37--0.43). A per-class error decomposition (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}, Table~\ref{tab:cross_llm_error}) confirms that true positive rates are identical across benign conditions; the F1 collapse is driven entirely by false positives on unfamiliar benign text. The residual cross-LLM degradation on structurally complex attacks (ActorAttack F1 = 0.80 vs.\ 0.997 in-distribution) indicates that some jailbreak features remain source-specific. Why MLM effectiveness is LLM-specific remains an open question; one hypothesis is that different LLMs produce stylistically distinct text whose surface patterns MLM captures via token co-occurrence, while classification supervision extracts features that generalize across styles. Mixed-LLM training partially resolves this gap: classification-based DAPT on Qwen+Llama data maintains OOD advantage (DD F1\,=\,0.818$\pm$0.096 vs.\ vanilla 0.291$\pm$0.065; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}), though multi-turn human evaluation remains open.

\paragraph{Lexical separability.}
TF-IDF logistic regression achieves F1 = 0.961 on OOD Deceptive Delight with near-perfect per-prefix performance; this indicates substantial lexical separability on this attack family. On DD alone, we cannot distinguish whether continued pretraining captures beyond-lexical features or exploits the same lexical cues. ActorAttack resolves this question: TF-IDF collapses to F1 = 0.261 while domain-adapted models maintain F1 $\geq$ 0.89, a $\geq$63\,pp gap that demonstrates continued pretraining captures features beyond lexical overlap on structurally complex attacks. Whether this beyond-lexical advantage extends to other structurally complex attack families beyond ActorAttack requires further evaluation.

\paragraph{Strategy annotation quality.}
Persuasion strategy labels are produced by Qwen3-8B with structured prompting. Inter-annotator agreement with a human sample ($n$=50) yields Cohen's $\kappa$=0.78, which indicates substantial but imperfect agreement. The small human validation sample and reliance on a single annotator model limit confidence in label quality. We plan to release the annotation prompts and data to enable independent verification.

\paragraph{OOD strategy prediction.}
While the 9-class model achieves high detection F1 on OOD attacks (0.997$\pm$0.005 on Deceptive Delight), its strategy-level predictions do not generalize: OOD strategy accuracy is 30.7\% ($\kappa$=0.126). The interpretability provided by the persuasion taxonomy is currently limited to known attack families where strategy labels are reliable.

\paragraph{FITD discriminative power.}
FITD provides minimal discriminative signal: both vanilla and 9-class achieve F1 $\geq$ 0.990 at all prefixes. FITD serves primarily as a confirmation that simple OOD attacks do not challenge any variant, rather than as a meaningful evaluation axis.

\paragraph{Encoder scale.}
Whether these findings hold at LLM-scale encoders (7B+) remains open; the capacity-dependent observation suggests larger pretrained models may require less or no domain adaptation. Confirming this requires same-architecture scaling with matched pretraining (e.g., DeBERTa-base vs.\ DeBERTa-large).

\paragraph{Topic variant anomaly.}
Topic classification occasionally approaches domain-adapted performance on ActorAttack (0.625 vs.\ MLM 0.893) despite using misaligned labels. This reflects domain exposure from processing jailbreak text, not the topic objective itself; extreme variance (std\,=\,0.141--0.207) confirms that misaligned supervision amplifies initialization sensitivity.

\paragraph{Scope.}
Only persuasion-mediated multi-turn attacks fall within scope. Single-turn token-level attacks~\citep{zou2023gcg}, non-persuasive multi-turn strategies, and attacks targeting modalities other than text are not addressed. The user-only input assumption (excluding assistant responses) may miss information in attacks where the model's own responses contribute to the escalation trajectory.

\paragraph{Single-turn transfer.}
ToxicChat jailbreaks are exclusively single-turn, while all training data consists of multi-turn conversations (5--14 turns). ToxicChat performance therefore reflects zero-shot single-turn transfer, not standard multi-turn OOD evaluation. The temporal aggregator can learn sequence length as a spurious feature: doubling the training corpus from 500 to 1{,}000 conversations drops 9-class ToxicChat macro F1 from 0.596 to 0.341$\pm$0.004, while multi-turn OOD detection remains stable (DD\,=\,0.889, ActorAttack\,=\,0.913). This length-dependent fragility confirms that the architecture targets multi-turn detection; single-turn robustness requires architectural changes beyond this work's scope.

\paragraph{Computation cost.}
Stage~1 continued pretraining requires approximately 30 minutes on a single A100 GPU per variant. Stage~2 training completes in under 5 minutes. Total pipeline cost for all seven variants is under 4 GPU-hours, making the approach accessible for replication.

% introduction.tex — expanded for 8-page target

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Multi-turn jailbreak attacks distribute adversarial intent across individually benign turns. Crescendo~\citep{russinovich2024crescendo}, Foot-in-the-Door~\citep{fitd2024}, and Deceptive Delight~\citep{deceptivedelight2024} all rely on persuasion as their core mechanism: \citet{zeng2024pap} catalogue 40 persuasion techniques across jailbreak families, and \citet{hagendorff2026reasoning} identify strategies in 97\% of successful attacks. Because individual attack turns occupy the same embedding region as benign text~\citep{bullwinkel2025repeng}, no single-turn filter can distinguish them from legitimate conversation.

Existing defenses leave a critical gap. Production classifiers such as Llama Guard~\citep{inan2023llamaguard}, WildGuard~\citep{han2024wildguard}, and ShieldGemma~\citep{zeng2024shieldgemma} operate on individual turns and therefore miss manipulation that unfolds across exchanges. Multi-turn defenses have begun to model conversation-level structure: DeepContext~\citep{deepcontext2026} pairs fine-tuned BERT with a multi-layer GRU, BIID~\citep{tong2025biid} infers turn-level intentions, and G-Guard~\citep{gguard2025} applies graph neural networks. These systems achieve strong in-distribution performance, but each is evaluated on a single attack family. None answers the central question: \emph{what representation properties enable a detector to generalize across attack families it has never seen?}

The simplest hypothesis is that pretrained language understanding already suffices. It does not, at least at base model scale. A vanilla DeBERTa-v3~\citep{he2021deberta} with temporal aggregation (cf.\ DeepContext) drops from F1\,=\,1.000 in-distribution to 0.312 on out-of-distribution (OOD) Deceptive Delight. LLM-as-judge approaches fare no better: GPT-5.5 reaches only 0.191--0.449 F1 with 30--47\% unparseable responses (Appendix~\ref{sec:llm_judge}). These failures share a root cause: neither model has been exposed to multi-turn adversarial text. The bottleneck is distributional, not architectural: cross-attack generalization requires domain adaptation, not a better classifier.

We test this hypothesis through controlled ablation. Our pipeline, \textbf{DisPeD} (Disentangled Persuasion Detection), separates encoder adaptation (Stage~1) from temporal classification (Stage~2, a frozen BiGRU; Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}). Seven Stage~1 variants each isolate a single factor (e.g., domain exposure, label quality, temporal modeling) while holding all other components constant (Section~\ref{sec:method}). The contribution is the systematic decomposition of what enables cross-attack generalization, not the pipeline itself.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_architecture.pdf}
\caption{DisPeD architecture. Stage~1 adapts a DeBERTa encoder on jailbreak conversation text (seven variant objectives). Stage~2 aggregates frozen \texttt{[CLS]} embeddings via BiGRU for conversation-level jailbreak detection. Only user turns are processed.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

Controlled ablation across DeBERTa-v3 and RoBERTa~\citep{liu2019roberta} on four attack families and two training compositions shows that for base-size encoders (125--184M), classification-based adaptation is the most robust variant across synthetic multi-turn OOD attacks. Continued pretraining closes $\sim$60\,pp on single-source data but collapses on mixed-LLM training (DD OOD F1\,=\,0.818 vs.\ 0.291). This dependence diminishes with scale: RoBERTa-large (355M) handles even ActorAttack~\citep{ren2024actorattack} without adaptation. Classification supervision reduces variance 4--20$\times$ on synthetic data but not on human-authored conversations (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}).

Our contributions:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=2pt, topsep=4pt]
  \item \textbf{Systematic decomposition of cross-attack generalization.} Controlled ablation across thirteen encoder configurations, three OOD attack families, and two training compositions identifies classification-based domain adaptation as the most robust variant within synthetic multi-turn evaluation; unsupervised pretraining is effective on single-source data but collapses on mixed-LLM training. Three negative results are counter-intuitive: scrambled labels match correct labels, mean pooling matches BiGRU, and label granularity does not affect detection.

  \item \textbf{Capacity-dependent observation on domain adaptation.} Three encoder scales (125M, 184M, 355M) show that the set of attacks requiring adaptation shrinks with model size; RoBERTa-large handles structurally complex attacks without any adaptation (Section~\ref{sec:cross_encoder}).

  \item \textbf{Condition-dependent adaptation effects.} Classification supervision reduces cross-seed variance 4--20$\times$ on synthetic data, but this benefit reverses on human-authored data. Mixed-LLM training validates that classification-based DAPT remains the strongest variant across training compositions (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}). The 9-class taxonomy enables interpretable strategy characterization (Section~\ref{sec:strategy}).
\end{itemize}

